.TH AVR\-GROUP\-DELETE 1 "" "avr" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-group-delete \- Delete an empty group.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr group delete
[OPTIONS] GROUP
.SH DESCRIPTION
Delete an empty group.
.PP
GROUP is a group ID (grp-...), the group's path from the root group, such as Organization/Platform/CI, or the end of a path when only one group has it, such as Platform/CI.
.PP
Asks first unless --yes is passed. A group that still has subgroups, members, a service token that is not revoked or expired, VMs its service tokens created, a vCPU limit, suspended members or workflows whose jobs are charged to it is not deleted; the error names what to clear, at most 100 workflows at a time.
.PP
Deleting a group also removes the resource access it grants, and stops charging it for workflows of repositories that are no longer installed.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
Examples:
    avr group delete Platform/CI
    avr group delete grp-0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef --yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
JSON FIELDS
    deleted, group_id, path
.fi
.RE
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIGROUP\fR
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR, \fB\-o\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID or slug. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Delete without asking.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR \fITEXT\fR
Output JSON. Pass comma-separated field names, "*" for all fields, or "?" to list available fields.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-jq\fR \fITEXT\fR
Filter --json output through a jq expression.
